Last months I had the pleasure of collaborating with Dr Brett Lewis and Professor Scott Bryan and his team on the 3D reconstruction of underwater coral ecosystems.

🪸🌊 Specifically we (Emilio Olivastri and me) got access to video footage from the project "Restoring Blast-Damaged Sloping Reefs with Community-Driven, Low-Cost 3D Printed Solutions" conducted by Tropical Research And Conservation Centre (TRACC) and lead by Robin Philippo.

🌏 The problem: Blast fishing across Southeast Asia shatters reef frameworks into unstable rubble on slopes of 15°–45°, making natural coral recovery virtually impossible. On Pom Pom Island (Malaysia), less than 5% coral cover remains — decades of damage caused in seconds.

🐚 Tropical Research And Conservation Centre (TRACC) is fighting back with an elegant, low-cost solution: modular, 3D-printed reef structures made from locally available cement and sand. Each mould can cast 1,000+ modules — potentially creating over 3,125 m² of new substrate for corals to settle and grow on sloping reefs.

Project: https://lnkd.in/gjtZBKt2
PADI: https://lnkd.in/gsxdVCeu

After some calibration and image undistortion preprocessing I ran our latest system, AllFeature-VSLAM, which tightly couples multiple types of features (ORB and SuperPoint in this case).

